# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .gitattributes
#
# This file lives at the ROOT of the repository because the rules apply to the
# whole tree - both terraform/ and tools/. In particular, it must keep covering
# the Image Builder components in terraform/images/components/*.yml, whose
# content is read by file() and becomes a resource attribute (see below).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS - incident of 2026-10-16.
#
# The rebase of the first push rewrote the working tree, and
# core.autocrlf = true (Git's default on Windows) converted the components'
# YAML from LF to CRLF.
#
# Terraform reads those files with file() and dumps the content into the
# "data" attribute of aws_imagebuilder_component. In a component EVERY argument
# is ForceNew, so the byte change turned into a REPLACEMENT - and it cascaded:
# a replaced component changes the ARN, the ARN is part of the recipe, so the
# three recipes changed too. The plan went from "No changes" to:
#
#   Plan: 9 to add, 5 to change, 6 to destroy
#
# Applying that would fail midway: the three AMIs reference the recipes, and
# removing a referenced recipe returns ResourceDependencyException. With
# create_before_destroy the removal comes last, so the apply would leave a new
# resource created and the old one stuck, in a partial state.
#
# THE WORST DETAIL: "git status" showed the tree CLEAN. Git normalizes line
# endings when comparing, so it does not see a difference - and Terraform does.
# Two tools reading the same file and disagreeing about the content.
#
# THE GENERAL RULE: a file whose CONTENT becomes a resource attribute cannot
# depend on a machine's checkout configuration. Here the risk class is exactly
# that of files read by file() or templatefile().
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Image Builder components (terraform/images/components/*.yml): the content
# goes into the "data" attribute.
# eol=lf guarantees LF in the working tree on any system, regardless of the
# core.autocrlf of whoever clones.
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# The .tf files are not read by file(), so CRLF in them does not replace a
# resource. Even so they stay in LF for consistency: it avoids noise diffs
# between whoever runs on Windows and the pipeline's Linux runner.
*.tf       text eol=lf
*.hcl      text eol=lf
*.md       text eol=lf
*.sql      text eol=lf
*.tfvars   text eol=lf

# Automatic detection for the rest, without forcing conversion on binaries.
* text=auto
